      "page_idx": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "Each landmark number is 10 times the previous one. Since 1 is the first landmark number, they are all powers of 10. The following are the symbols given to these numbers —"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.148,
            0.839,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": "| 1 | 102 10 | 103 10⁵ 104 | 10⁶ | 107 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_table_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.228,
            0.848,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": "As in the case of Roman numbers, a given number is counted in groups of the landmark numbers, starting from the largest landmark number less than the given number. This is then used to assign the numeral."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.283,
            0.848,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": "For example 324 which equals 100 + 100 + 100 + 10 + 10 + 4 is written as ."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.331,
            0.293,
            0.354
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.356,
            0.847,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "1. Represent the following numbers in the Egyptian system: 10458, 1023, 2660, 784, 1111, 70707."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.405,
            0.624,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": "2. What numbers do these numerals stand for?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.229,
            0.446,
            0.254,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "(i)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.44,
            0.372,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.571,
            0.446,
            0.603,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "(ii)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.611,
            0.439,
            0.768,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.574,
            0.849,
            0.617
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "II. Variations on the Egyptian System and the Notion of Base"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.627,
            0.848,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "？ Instead of grouping together 10 collections of size equal to the previous landmark number (as in the case of the Egyptian system), can we get a number system by grouping together 5 collections of size equal to the previous landmark number? Can this 5 be replaced by any positive integer?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.725,
            0.804,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "Let us examine this possibility. Let 1 be the first landmark number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.745,
            0.848,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": "Group together 5 collections of size equal to the previous landmark number (1). Its size is the second landmark number which is 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.781,
            0.848,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": "Group together 5 collections of size equal to the previous landmark number (5). Its size is the third landmark number which is 5 × 5 = 25."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.819,
            0.848,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": "Group together 5 collections of size equal to the previous landmark number (5). Its size is the fourth landmark number which is 5 × 25 = 125."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.915,
            0.12,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "62"
        },
        {
